\section1 Combat

\section3 Properties

The combat is a bit more complicated to implement. We first need the stats for the \c Player and enemies.\br
Those are handled by the managers, although the enemies still have their own properties, so they need those properties in the \c spawnEnemy function.\br
\c entities/Enemy.qml
\code
Entity {
    id: enemy
    ...
    
    property int currentHealth
    property int maxHealth
    property int damage
}
\endcode
\br

\c entities/EnemyManager.qml
\code
EntityManager {
    id: enemyManager
    
    ...

    property int defaultHealth: 5
    property int defaultDamage: 1

    property int health: defaultHealth
    property int damage: defaultDamage

    ...

    function spawnEnemy(playerTileIndex, tileIndex) {
        const enemyPostion = tilemap.tileIndexToPosition(tileIndex.row,tileIndex.column);
        tilemap.setEntityTileOccupation(tileIndex.row,tileIndex.column);
        var newEntityProperties = {
            row: tileIndex.row,
            column: tileIndex.column,
            imgSize: tilemap.tileSize,
            x: enemyPostion.x,
            y: enemyPostion.y,
            currentHealth: enemyManager.health,
            maxHealth: enemyManager.health,
            damage: enemyManager.damage
        }

        const enemyId = enemyManager.createEntityFromUrlWithProperties(
                                    Qt.resolvedUrl("Enemy.qml"),
                                    newEntityProperties);
        const enemy = getLastAddedEntity();
        enemy.finishedAnimation.connect(enemyAnimationDone);
        enemies.push({id: enemyId, enemy: enemy})
        entityContainer.changeVisualEntityOrder();
    }

    ...
}
\endcode
\br

\c entities/PlayerManager.qml
\code
EntityManager {
    id: playerManager

    ...

    property int defaultHealth: 8
    property int defaultDamage: 3

    property int currentHealth: defaultHealth
    property int maxHealth: defaultHealth
    property int damage: defaultDamage

    ...
}
\endcode
\br

\section3 Attack Animation

With that done we need the rest of the attack logic.\br
We first have the attack animation of the \c Entity. \br
\c entities/Entity.qml
\code

EntityBase {
    id: entity

    ...
    
    property int attackSpeed: 8

    ...

    function attack(targetX, targetY) {
        entity.continousAnimation = true;
        const currentX = entity.x;
        const currentY = entity.y;

        const xInBetween =  currentX + (targetX - currentX) * 0.65;
        const yInBetween =  currentY + (targetY - currentY) * 0.65;

        entity.moveTo(xInBetween,yInBetween,entity.attackSpeed);

        const f = () => {
            entity.moveTo(currentX,currentY,entity.attackSpeed);
            entity.continousAnimation = false;
            entity.finshedPartAnimation.disconnect(f);
        }
        entity.finshedPartAnimation.connect(f);
    }
}
\endcode
\br

\section3 Player Attack Logic

In the \c PlayerManager we need to extend the \c playerActionToTile function with the attack logic.\br
We also need to implement the \c attackEnemy and \c attackPlayer function.\br
At last we need to be able to reset the \c Player properties.\br
\c entities/PlayerManager.qml
\code
EntityManager {
    id: playerManager

    ...

    signal playerDied()

    ...

    function playerActionToTile(row,column) { // returns {row, column} of next tile
        playerManager.targetTileIndex = {row:row,column:column};
        if(player.row === row && player.column === column) {
            playerManager.finishedTurn();
            return {row,column: column};
        }

        if(playerManager.isNeighboringTileToPlayer(row,column)) {
            if(tilemap.tiles[row][column].occupied) {
                const killed = playerManager.attackEnemy(row,column)
                if(killed) {
                    playerManager.movePlayerToTileIndex(row,column,true);
                    playerManager.finishTurnAfterAnimation();
                    return {row: player.row,column: player.column};
                } else {
                    const position = tilemap.tileIndexToPosition(row,column);
                    player.attack(position.x,position.y,player.attackSpeed);
                    playerManager.finishTurnAfterAnimation();
                    return {row: player.row,column: player.column}
                }
            } else {
                playerManager.movePlayerToTileIndex(row,column);
                playerManager.finishTurnAfterAnimation();
                return {row: player.row,column: player.column};
            }
        }
        const tileIndex = movePlayerTowards(row,column);
        return tileIndex;
    }

    ...

    function attackEnemy(row,column) { // returns true if enemy has been killed and the player should move
        return enemyManager.attackEnemy(playerManager.damage,row,column);
    }

    function attackPlayer(damage) {
        playerManager.currentHealth -= damage;
        if(playerManager.currentHealth <= 0) {
            playerManager.playerDied();
        }
    }

    function resetPlayerProperties() {
        playerManager.currentHealth = playerManager.defaultHealth;
        playerManager.maxHealth = playerManager.defaultHealth;
        playerManager.damage = playerManager.defaultDamage;
    }
}
\endcode
\br

\section3 Enemy Attack Logic

In the  \c EnemyManager we need to extend the \c enemyActionToTile function with the attack logic.\br
We also need to implement the \c attackEnemy function which will be called from the \c PlayerManager. \br
At last we need to be able to reset the \c Enemy properties.\br
\c entities/EnemyManager.qml
\code
EntityManager {
    id: enemyManager

    ...
    signal attackPlayer(damage: int)
    ...

    function enemyActionToTile(enemy,row,column) {
        if(enemyManager.isNeighboringTile(enemy,row,column)) {
            const position = tilemap.tileIndexToPosition(row,column);
            enemyManager.pendingAnimations++;
            enemy.attack(position.x,position.y,enemy.attackSpeed);
            enemyManager.attackPlayer(enemy.damage);
        } else {
            enemyManager.moveEnemyTowards(enemy,row,column);
        }
    }

    ...

    function attackEnemy(damage,row,column) { // returns true if enemy has been killed
        const enemyProperties = enemyManager.enemyAtPosition(row,column);
        if(enemyProperties !== null) {
            const {id, enemy} = enemyProperties;
            enemy.currentHealth -= damage;
            if(enemy.currentHealth <= 0) {
                enemyManager.removeEnemy(enemy,id)
                return true;
            }
        }
        return false;
    }

    function resetEnemyProperties() {
        enemyManager.pendingAnimations = 0;
        enemyManager.health = enemyManager.defaultHealth;
        enemyManager.damage = enemyManager.defaultDamage;
        enemyManager.enemyCount = enemyManager.defaultEnemyCount;
    }
}
\endcode
\br

\section3 Integration

In the \c DungeonScene we need to add the reset properties functions from \c PlayerManager and \c EnemyManager and let the enemies be able to attack the \c Player. \br
We also need to connect the \c playerDied signal with the \c GameOverScene. \br
\c scenes/DungeonScene.qml
\code
Scene {
    id: dungeonScene

    ...

    signal playerDied()

    ...

    onVisibleChanged: {
        if (visible) {
            playerManager.resetPlayerProperties();
            enemyManager.resetEnemyProperties();
            dungeonScene.createMap();
        } else {
            enemyManager.clearEnemies();
        }
    }

    Item {
        ...
        EnemyManager {
            ...
            onAttackPlayer: (damage) => playerManager.attackPlayer(damage)
            ...
        }

        PlayerManager {
            ...
            onPlayerDied: dungeonScene.playerDied()
            ...
        }
    }

    ...
}
\endcode
\br

To change to the \c GameOverScene on death we need to connect the signal.\br
\c Main.qml
\code
GameWindow {
    id: gameWindow

    ...

    DungeonScene {
        ...
        onPlayerDied: gameWindow.changeScene(gameOverScene)
    }

    ...
}
\endcode
\br

\section3 Enemy Healthbar

Now there should be working attack movement.\br
But the \c Enemy Healthbar is still missing.\br
\c entities/EnemyHealthBar.qml
\code
import QtQuick
import Felgo

Rectangle {
    id: healthBar
    color: "red"
    property int value
    property int max

    Rectangle {
        color: "#0f0"
        anchors.top: parent.top
        anchors.bottom: parent.bottom
        width: parent.width * (value / max)
    }
}
\endcode

And it needs to be added to the \c Enemy. \br
\c entities/Enemy.qml
\code
Entity {
    id: enemy
    
    ...

    EnemyHealthBar {
        id: healthBar
        visible: enemy.currentHealth < enemy.maxHealth
        width: enemy.imgSize * 0.8
        height: enemy.imgSize / 10
        x: -healthBar.width/ 2
        y: -enemy.imgSize / 3 * 2 - healthBar.height*2
        value: enemy.currentHealth
        max: enemy.maxHealth
    }
}
\endcode
\br

